{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>My spirit shall not abide in man forever.</b> The word ידון is derived from נדנה, concerning which it is written (Daniel 7:15) ״אתכריית רוחי אנא דניאל בגו נדנה״, and נדנה is explained as a sheath (תיק). And the sheath of a sword is called נדנה, and is called תיק. And likewise the body is a תיק and a vessel for the soul. Therefore the verse says: My spirit — that is, the rational soul which I gave from My spirit — shall not make a sheath in man forever, that is, it shall not endure in him forever, because he is not worthy of that spirit." "And he explained the reason in <b>in that he is flesh (בשגם הוא בשר)</b>: that man too is flesh like all flesh that creeps upon the earth — bird, beast, and wild animal — for the spirit which I placed in him is intellect, yet they follow after flesh and desires, not after intellect." "<b>And his days shall be a hundred and twenty years.</b> That is, because of this I will lengthen My anger toward them and fix for them a hundred and twenty years that they may return in repentance, and if they do not return in repentance they shall perish in the Flood. And Radak (the Kamchi) interpreted in his father's name: לא ידון is from the root מדון — he says: My rational spirit shall not be in strife (מריבה) with man forever, for My spirit has no dealings with desires while the body is drawn after them; this is what it says: בשגם הוא בשר." "<b>And it appears</b> to me regarding the reason for this number: that because this world was created with the letter ה, and He desires the endurance of the world, therefore He waited for them a hundred and twenty years — for a hundred is one-fifth of the years of the span of the Tree of Life, so that they might contemplate and return. And likewise twenty is one-fifth of a hundred, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not punish until twenty. And likewise in the future, of which it is written (Isaiah 65:22): ״כי כימי העץ ימי עמי״ — He does not punish until a hundred; and thus it is written: ״כי הנער בן מאה שנה ימות״, and so forth. And this is the sense of והיו ימיו — that is, as though they are born today and their flesh is moist from youth." "<b>In that he is flesh (בשגם הוא בשר).</b> Moses is alluded to here, and likewise the number of his years. And they said in the Midrash that Noah was not worthy of being saved, since it is written (Genesis 6:7): ״כי נחמתי כי עשיתים״, but rather he was saved by the merit of Moses, who was destined to come forth from him — and this is the allusion in בשגם."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "הקמחי resolved as Radak (R. David Kimhi), following the explicit attribution 'in his father's name' (R. Joseph Kimhi).", 3 "'רוטפש' appears to be a typographical corruption, likely for ירטב (rendered here hyper-literally: 'their flesh is moist'). The phrase echoes rabbinic language of rejuvenation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:47.739192007Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}